+---
+id: task-005-create-a-json-patch-from-two-versions-createpatch
+type: task
+title: "Create a JSON Patch from two versions (createPatch)"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — Create a JSON Patch from two versions (createPatch)
+
+## Request
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the whole new
+version. Add a way to produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902).
+
+Export a function `createPatch(from, to)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns a JSON Patch that turns `from`
+into `to` when applied, and must not mutate either input.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. Applying the returned patch to `from` with `applyPatch` yields a document equal to `to`, for any pair of
+   JSON values.
+2. Equal inputs give an empty patch.
+3. The patch describes what changed, and nothing more: when two versions differ in one small place, the
+   patch is small, and the whole new document is not sent again.
+4. Object members that are only in `from` are removed; members only in `to` are added; members in both are
+   compared recursively.
+5. Arrays: when an item is added or removed, the patch adds or removes that item, and does not rewrite the
+   items around it. When an item moves within an array, the patch removes it and adds it back (the spec
+   accepts either a move or a remove and add), and does not rewrite the items in between.
+6. An array item that changed in place, such as one field of an object, is patched within the item, when
+   that is smaller than replacing the item.
+7. A value whose type changed (for example a number to an object, or an array to an object) is replaced.
+8. The inputs are never mutated, and the patch shares no mutable structure with either input.
+9. Inputs that are not JSON values (`undefined`, non-finite numbers, class instances, cycles, holes in
+   arrays) throw an error, as `applyMergePatch` does.
+10. A member named `__proto__` is an ordinary member name.
+11. The implementation has no runtime dependency. The README documents the function, next to `applyPatch`.
+
+## Test classification
+
+- Happy paths (object member add, remove and change; array add, remove and move; in-place change of an
+  array item): new behaviour, so these tests are red-first.
+- Edge and error paths: each criterion 1 to 11 has at least one test.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- `createPatch(from, to)` is exported from `src/index.ts`. Objects are diffed member by member. Arrays are
+  aligned on their shared items (common prefix and suffix, then a longest common subsequence of the items'
+  canonical forms); within each gap between shared items, items are paired in order and patched in place
+  when that is smaller than replacing them, and the leftovers are removed or added.
+- A moved item is patched as a remove and an add, which the request allows; the `move` operation is not used.
+- Tests: `test/create-patch.test.ts` (21 tests, written red first, then green). `npm test`: 102 of 102 pass.
+- A randomized check of 20,000 document pairs (outside the repository) round-trips through `applyPatch`
+  and leaves the inputs unchanged.
+- The README documents the function next to `applyPatch`.
+- Limitation: the alignment table is quadratic in the length of the array middle that changed between the
+  shared prefix and suffix, so two very long, heavily edited arrays use memory proportional to their product.
